## JavaScript (Node.js / Browser)

### Cooperative yielding with `async/await`
```javascript
async function lcsAsync(s1, s2, yieldEvery = 1000) {
    const m = s1.length, n = s2.length;
    // Create DP table
    const dp = Array.from({ length: m + 1 }, () => new Array(n + 1).fill(0));

    let counter = 0;
    for (let i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
        for (let j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (s1[i - 1] === s2[j - 1]) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }

            counter++;
            if (counter % yieldEvery === 0) {
                // Yield to the event loop
                await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 0));
            }
        }
    }

    // Reconstruct the LCS
    let i = m, j = n;
    const lcs = [];
    while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
        if (s1[i - 1] === s2[j - 1]) {
            lcs.push(s1[i - 1]);
            i--; j--;
        } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {
            i--;
        } else {
            j--;
        }
    }

    return {
        sequence: lcs.reverse().join(""),
        length: dp[m][n]
    };
}

// Usage
(async () => {
    const { sequence, length } = await lcsAsync("AGGTAB", "GXTXAYB");
    console.log(`LCS: ${sequence} (length ${length})`);
})();
```

---

## Which approach to choose?

- **Thread offloading** (`run_in_executor` in Python, `worker_threads` in Node) is the easiest way to avoid blocking the event loop. It is ideal when you are already using a thread‑pool or when the strings are very large (because you get true parallelism on multi‑core systems).
- **Cooperative yielding** stays entirely within a single thread and avoids thread‑safety issues, but it may still saturate the CPU between yields. It’s most useful for lightweight async frameworks or when you cannot spawn threads (e.g., browser main thread, some serverless environments).

Both implementations return the actual longest common subsequence **and** its length, so you can use whichever fits your application’s concurrency model.

