## 3. Benefits of the Builder Pattern

| Without Builder | With Builder |
|----------------|--------------|
| Construction logic scattered; every creation site repeats boilerplate. | Centralised construction – change defaults or validation in one place. |
| No compile‑time or runtime guidance on mandatory fields. | Builder can check constraints in `build()` (e.g., name must be set). |
| Hard to create preset configurations (e.g., `create_warrior_preset()`). | The same builder can be handed to a **Director** that calls a sequence of setters to produce different representations. |
| Memory management mixed with object setup. | Builder manages its own temporary storage; the final product is allocated atomically in `build()`. |
| Code is verbose and error‑prone (forgetting `strdup`, wrong array size). | Fluent interface (`->set_*`) reads naturally and is less prone to omission. |

The Builder pattern is especially valuable in game engines where components (particle systems, AI profiles, UI widgets) have dozens of configurable parameters – many optional – and need to be created in varied states without exploding the number of factory functions.

