# Refactoring a God Object: Replacing Magic Values with Enums and Value Objects

## The Problem

A 1281‑line Java class that fetches data, transforms it, caches results, validates inputs, and renders HTML UI is a classic **God Object** – it knows too much and does too much. One of the prime contributors to this bloat is the heavy use of **magic numbers and strings** scattered throughout the code. These literal values obscure the domain, make the code brittle, and prevent the natural extraction of smaller, focused classes.

Below we illustrate how **typed enumerations** and **value objects** can replace those magic values, dramatically improving the design and setting the stage for breaking up the monolith.

---

## Before: The Bloated `DashboardService` (Excerpt)

```java
public class DashboardService {
    private Map<String, Object> cache = new HashMap<>();
    private static final int CACHE_TTL = 3600;                 // magic seconds
    private static final int MAX_RETRY = 3;
    private static final String CONFIG_CACHE_TTL = "cache.ttl";  // magic key
    private static final String CONFIG_MAX_RETRY = "max.retry";

    public String getUserDashboard(String userId) {
        Map<String, Object> userData = fetchUserFromDB(userId);
        if (userData == null) {
            return renderErrorPage(1001, "User not found");   // magic error code
        }

        int role = (int) userData.get("role");                // magic role integer
        String status = (String) userData.get("status");

        if (status == null || !"active".equals(status)) {     // magic status string
            return renderErrorPage(1002, "User is inactive");
        }

        // cache logic with magic TTL
        String cacheKey = "dashboard_" + userId;
        if (cache.containsKey(cacheKey)) {
            Map<String, Object> cached = (Map<String, Object>) cache.get(cacheKey);
            long timestamp = (long) cached.get("timestamp");
            if (System.currentTimeMillis() - timestamp < CACHE_TTL * 1000) {
                return (String) cached.get("html");
            }
        }

        String html = buildDashboardHtml(userData, role);
        Map<String, Object> cacheEntry = new HashMap<>();
        cacheEntry.put("timestamp", System.currentTimeMillis());
        cacheEntry.put("html", html);
        cache.put(cacheKey, cacheEntry);
        return html;
    }

    private String buildDashboardHtml(Map<String, Object> userData, int role) {
        String name = (String) userData.get("name");
        String type = (String) userData.get("type"); // "premium", "basic"
        StringBuilder html = new StringBuilder();
        html.append("<div style='color: #333333;'>");        // magic color

        if (role == 1) {                                    // magic role 1
            html.append("<h1>Admin Dashboard</h1>");
            html.append("<p>Welcome, ").append(name).append(" (Admin)</p>");
        } else if (role == 2) {                             // magic role 2
            html.append("<h1>User Dashboard</h1>");
            if ("premium".equals(type)) {                   // magic type string
                html.append("<p style='color: #FFD700;'>Premium features</p>");
            }
        } else if (role == 3) {                             // magic role 3
            html.append("<h1>Guest Dashboard</h1>");
        }
        // ... more inline styling and magic strings
        return html.toString();
    }

    private String renderErrorPage(int errorCode, String message) {
        if (errorCode == 1001) {
            return "<html><body><h1 style='color: #FF0000;'>Not Found</h1></body></html>";
        } else if (errorCode == 1002) {
            return "<html><body><h1 style='color: #FF0000;'>Inactive</h1></body></html>";
        }
        // ...
    }
    // ... hundreds of lines more
}
```

Every literal value (`1`, `"active"`, `"premium"`, `1001`, `#333333`, `"cache.ttl"`) is a **magic value** – its meaning is implicit, duplicated, and fragile.

---

## After: Refactored with Enums and Value Objects

### Step 1: Introduce Domain Enums for Fixed Sets of Values

```java
public enum Role {
    ADMIN(1, "Admin"),
    USER(2, "User"),
    GUEST(3, "Guest");

    private final int id;
    private final String displayName;

    Role(int id, String displayName) {
        this.id = id;
        this.displayName = displayName;
    }

    public boolean isAdmin() { return this == ADMIN; }
    public String getDisplayName() { return displayName; }

    public static Role fromId(int id) {
        for (Role r : values()) if (r.id == id) return r;
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid role id: " + id);
    }
}

public enum AccountStatus {
    ACTIVE("active"), INACTIVE("inactive");
    private final String value;
    AccountStatus(String value) { this.value = value; }
    public static AccountStatus fromString(String s) {
        for (AccountStatus st : values()) if (st.value.equals(s)) return st;
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown status: " + s);
    }
}

public enum UserType {
    BASIC("basic"), PREMIUM("premium");
    private final String value;
    UserType(String value) { this.value = value; }
}
```

### Step 2: Replace Magic Error Codes and Colors with Descriptive Enums

```java
public enum ErrorCode {
    USER_NOT_FOUND(1001, "User not found", 404),
    USER_INACTIVE(1002, "User is inactive", 403);

    private final int code;
    private final String defaultMessage;
    private final int httpStatus;

    ErrorCode(int code, String defaultMessage, int httpStatus) { ... }
    // getters...
    public String render(String customMessage) {
        return String.format("<h1 style='color: %s;'>%s</h1>", 
                             HtmlColor.RED.getHex(), 
                             customMessage != null ? customMessage : defaultMessage);
    }
}

public enum HtmlColor {
    DARK_GRAY("#333333"), RED("#FF0000"), GOLD("#FFD700");
    private final String hex;
    HtmlColor(String hex) { this.hex = hex; }
    public String getHex() { return hex; }
}

public enum ConfigKey {
    CACHE_TTL("cache.ttl", "3600"),
    MAX_RETRY("max.retry", "3");
    private final String key;
    private final String defaultValue;
    // ...
}
```

### Step 3: Create Value Objects for Cohesive Data and Configuration

```java
// Immutable value object replacing raw user Map
public class User {
    private final String id;
    private final String name;
    private final Role role;
    private final AccountStatus status;
    private final UserType type;
    // constructor, getters...
}

// Value object for cache configuration
public class CacheConfig {
    private final long ttlSeconds;
    private final int maxSize;

    public CacheConfig(long ttlSeconds, int maxSize) { ... }
    public long getTtlMillis() { return ttlSeconds * 1000; }
}
```

### Step 4: Refactored `DashboardService` (Now Thin and Focused)

```java
public class DashboardService {
    private final Map<String, CacheEntry> cache = new HashMap<>();
    private final CacheConfig cacheConfig;
    private final UserRepository userRepo;   // extracted dependency

    public DashboardService(CacheConfig cacheConfig, UserRepository userRepo) {
        this.cacheConfig = cacheConfig;
        this.userRepo = userRepo;
    }

    public String getUserDashboard(String userId) {
        User user = userRepo.findById(userId);
        if (user == null) {
            return ErrorCode.USER_NOT_FOUND.render(null);
        }
        if (user.getStatus() != AccountStatus.ACTIVE) {
            return ErrorCode.USER_INACTIVE.render(null);
        }

        String cacheKey = "dashboard_" + userId;
        if (cache.containsKey(cacheKey)) {
            CacheEntry entry = cache.get(cacheKey);
            if (!entry.isExpired(cacheConfig.getTtlMillis())) {
                return entry.getHtml();
            }
        }

        String html = buildDashboardHtml(user);
        cache.put(cacheKey, new CacheEntry(html));
        return html;
    }

    private String buildDashboardHtml(User user) {
        StringBuilder html = new StringBuilder();
        html.append("<div style='color:").append(HtmlColor.DARK_GRAY.getHex()).append(";'>");

        if (user.getRole().isAdmin()) {
            html.append("<h1>Admin Dashboard</h1>");
        } else if (user.getRole() == Role.USER) {
            html.append("<h1>User Dashboard</h1>");
            if (user.getType() == UserType.PREMIUM) {
                html.append("<p style='color:").append(HtmlColor.GOLD.getHex()).append(";'>Premium features</p>");
            }
        } else {
            html.append("<h1>Guest Dashboard</h1>");
        }
        // ...
        return html.toString();
    }
}
```

---

## Improvements and Benefits

| Magic Value Replaced | New Type | Benefit |
|----------------------|----------------|---------|
| Role integers (`1`,`2`,`3`) | `Role` enum | **Type safety** prevents invalid roles; **self‑documenting** code (`Role.ADMIN` vs `1`); business logic like `isAdmin()` lives on the enum, shrinking the God Object. |
| Status strings (`"active"`) | `AccountStatus` enum | Eliminates string duplication and typos; centralises conversion from DB strings to types; enables safer method signatures (`User.getStatus()` returns `AccountStatus`, not `String`). |
| User type strings (`"premium"`) | `UserType` enum | Conditionals become explicit (`user.getType() == UserType.PREMIUM`), reducing bugs. |
| Error codes (`1001`, `1002`) | `ErrorCode` enum | Each error bundles its HTTP status, message, and even **rendering behaviour** – UI generation moves out of the service, directly fighting the God Object. |
| HTML colour strings (`"#333333"`) | `HtmlColor` enum | Colours are named and centrally managed; a design change updates one place, not dozens. |
| Configuration keys | `ConfigKey` enum | Keys are no longer raw strings; default values and documentation live with the key, making configuration self‑describing. |
| Cache TTL | `CacheConfig` value object | Encapsulates TTL conversion logic (`getTtlMillis()`); can be validated on construction; easily reused and tested independently. |
| User data (`Map<String,Object>`) | `User` value object | Conveys **domain meaning**; ensures required fields exist; transports validation and formatting logic away from the service. |

### Additional Benefits

- **Easier Maintenance & Extension**  
  Adding a new role (e.g., `MODERATOR`) now means adding one enum constant; IDEs will automatically flag all switch/if statements that need attention (especially with `switch` expressions). Without enums, you must remember every `if (role == ...)` scattered across 1200 lines.

- **Readability and Communication**  
  `if (user.getStatus() == AccountStatus.INACTIVE)` tells a story. `if ("inactive".equals(status))` does not – it forces the reader to know which strings are valid and what they mean.

- **Natural Distribution of Responsibility**  
  By moving behaviour into enums and value objects (e.g., `ErrorCode.render()`, `Role.isAdmin()`), the God Object sheds weight. What remains is coordination logic, not data validation or formatting.

- **Compile‑Time Safety**  
  Method signatures like `renderErrorPage(int code, String msg)` accept any integer; `renderErrorPage(ErrorCode code)` only accepts valid error codes. This catches mistakes at compile time rather than runtime.

- **Testability**  
  Enums and value objects are trivially testable in isolation. The main service can be tested with stubs that return proper `User` instances instead of fragile, hand‑crafted maps.

---

## Conclusion

Replacing magic values with typed enumerations and value objects is not a cosmetic change – it is a **structural refactoring** that directly attacks the God Object anti‑pattern. The new types act as attractors for related data and behaviour, naturally pulling responsibilities out of the monolithic class. The resulting code is safer, more expressive, and far easier to evolve. Once these foundational types are in place, further decompositions (extracting a `DashboardRenderer`, a `CacheManager`, etc.) become straightforward. The 1281‑line monster shrinks into a set of small, focused, and maintainable components.

