## Improvements & Benefits

| Improvement | Benefit |
|-------------|---------|
| **Linear, readable flow** | Async/await turns nested callbacks into a top‑to‑bottom sequence that matches the business logic. No more “callback‑hell”. |
| **Centralised error handling** | Exceptions can be caught in one `try/catch` block (or left to propagate). No risk of forgetting `return` after `callback(err)`. |
| **Easier to modify** | Adding/removing a step is a matter of inserting/removing one line – no re‑indenting or restructuring of callbacks. |
| **Improved testability** | The new function returns a Promise, which is native to modern test runners (e.g. `async/await` in tests). Mocking dependencies that return promises is simpler and more reliable. |
| **Incremental, safe migration** | The Strangler Fig pattern allowed us to replace the implementation without a risky “big‑bang” rewrite. Tests stayed green throughout, and we could roll back instantly by flipping a feature flag. |
| **Better separation of concerns** | Each step can easily be extracted into its own async function (e.g. `readAndParse`, `validateAndCheck`) later, promoting reusability. |

By applying the Strangler Fig pattern, we turned a fragile, deeply nested legacy function into a clean, maintainable async pipeline **without ever breaking the existing callers or tests**.

