The inequality \(x^2 < 7x\) can be rewritten as \(x^2 - 7x < 0\), which factors to \(x(x - 7) < 0\). The quadratic expression \(x(x - 7)\) is a parabola opening upward with zeros at \(x = 0\) and \(x = 7\). It is negative for values of \(x\) strictly between the roots, so the solution is \(0 < x < 7\).

The integer values that satisfy \(0 < x < 7\) are \(x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\). Thus, there are 6 integer solutions.

\boxed{6}

The triangle inequality states that the sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the length of the third side. For sides \(x\), \(5\), and \(6\), we have:

\[
\begin{aligned}
x + 5 &> 6 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x > 1, \\
x + 6 &> 5 \quad \Rightarrow \quad x > -1 \quad \text{(always true for positive }x\text{)}, \\
5 + 6 &> x \quad \Rightarrow \quad x < 11.
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus, \(x\) must satisfy \(1 < x < 11\). The possible integer values for \(x\) are \(2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10\). The greatest possible integer value is \(10\), and the least is \(2\). Their positive difference is \(10 - 2 = 8\).

\boxed{8}

The probability that Kim faces a math test is \(\frac{4}{7}\). The probability that she does not face a math test (and thus drafts maps peacefully) is the complement of this event:

\[
1 - \frac{4}{7} = \frac{3}{7}
\]

\boxed{\dfrac{3}{7}}
